  <span class="kicker">About</span>
  <h1>How we work, and what we won't claim</h1>
  <p class="meta">Method, limits and ground rules</p>

  <article>
    <p>Most of what gets sold as AI visibility work is a dashboard and a list of suggestions. We build the signals instead. This page explains how the research is produced, so you can judge the method before you hire anyone to apply it.</p>

    <h2>Why this company exists</h2>

    <p>Ask an answer engine what the best product in a category is, several times over, and the same handful of brands tends to come back. Not always the best brands. Often the ones whose evidence was easiest for a model to find, parse and reuse.</p>

    <p>That is a marketing problem, and for a while almost nobody treated it as one. A wave of tools arrived to measure it, which is the easy half. Measuring your own invisibility does not change it. Someone still has to go and build the reviews, the citations, the structured data and the community presence that change what the model says. That is the work.</p>

    <h2>How the panels are built</h2>

    <p>Every claim in our research should be checkable, so here is the procedure. A panel is four to five unbranded buyer questions for a category. Each question is run five times in fresh ChatGPT sessions with web search enabled and personalisation off. For every run we record whether the brand was named, whether it was named first, and which source domains were visible in the answer.</p>

    <p>Twenty runs is enough to see a pattern and not enough to prove a cause. A source appearing alongside a recommendation is a correlation, not an explanation of why the model chose that brand. We say so in the research, and we will say so to you rather than dress a pattern up as a mechanism.</p>

    <p>Where a brand's absence has an obvious innocent explanation, we publish that too. Sometimes a product genuinely is not the right answer to a question, and optimisation should not try to manufacture eligibility.</p>

    <h2>Read the research before you talk to us</h2>

    <p>The three teardowns on the blog are brands nobody paid us to analyse: <a href="/blog/improving-our-place-aeo/">Our Place</a>, <a href="/blog/improving-material-kitchen-aeo/">Material Kitchen</a> and <a href="/blog/improving-tower-28-aeo/">Tower 28</a>. Each one shows the prompts, the counts and the sources.</p>

    <p>Read one first. If the thinking does not hold up, you have spent ten minutes instead of a quarter's budget.</p>

    <h2>What we will not do</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Buy reviews, incentivise them without disclosure, or gate them. Fake and AI-generated reviews are illegal under the FTC's Consumer Review Rule, and the rule exists for good reason.</li>
      <li>Astroturf Reddit or any other community. Community participation happens under a disclosed identity or it does not happen.</li>
      <li>Promise a ranking inside ChatGPT. Nobody controls a base model's output, and anyone selling a position in one is selling something that does not exist.</li>
      <li>Report a number we cannot trace back to a saved answer.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Getting in touch</h2>

